SOAPBOX: Used weekend to get a basic station on the air from Cape Cod. Had a transceiver and some wire here. Most difficult items to locate were the correct microphone connector and a USB-to-ethernet converter to connect the Flex-6500 to my MacBook. Amazon overnight to the rescue. Then I had to download, configure, and learn the new
SOAPBOX: logging and SDR software on the fly. Part of the fun? In the old days erecting an antenna and hooking up the radio got one on the air. Now that is just the beginning of the process. The trade-offs are no hand-duping logs and writing QSLs.
SOAPBOX: The bigger problem was that the planned vertical wire antenna wouldn't resonate and the weather was freezing cold and windy.
SOAPBOX: On Sunday I finally put up an EFHW at 23 feet (7 meters). The serious competitors at big stations a skip or two away were easy to work and may have appreciated the new multiplier late Sunday. Others were difficult to work, although two JAs and a KH6 made it into the log on 15. And DXpedition CY0S was welcomingly loud single-skip on both 20 and 15.
